Id: lin014f
Severity: FAIL
Category: network
Check: check_network_config
Fix: Set net.ipv4.conf.all.rp_filter and .default.rp_filter to 1 (2 on asymmetric routes) in /etc/sysctl.d.
References: sysctl.d(5); Linux kernel ip-sysctl documentation; RFC 3704
Controls: CIS 4.1; NIST SC-5, SC-7; ISO 8.20; CE Secure configuration

An interface does no reverse path filtering: packets whose source
address no route leads back to are accepted, so spoofed packets reach
this host's services, and a compromised host here can send them.
Spoofed packets are what denial of service, man in the middle and
connection hijacking are built on. The kernel filters an interface by
the higher of net.ipv4.conf.all.rp_filter and the interface's own
value, so both are 0 for the interfaces named (new interfaces take
conf/default). Strict mode is 1 (lin051w says why loose mode, 2, is
less); Debian ships neither, Ubuntu, Fedora and Arch 2, Alpine 1:
  # sysctl -w net.ipv4.conf.all.rp_filter=1
and:
  # sysctl -w net.ipv4.conf.default.rp_filter=1
